WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.375
YURI GRINSHTEYN: The most
important feature of any system

00:00:02.375 --> 00:00:04.170
is its reliability.

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:06.780
This is the driving principle
behind cyber reliability

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.540
engineering as invented
and practiced at Google.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.820
This is because you can
easily lose the trust

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.080
and respect of your users.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.420
If your service
becomes too unreliable,

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:18.300
or is simply perceived
as being too unreliable,

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.880
those countless hours you've
spent designing and building

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.860
the most amazing
user experiences

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:26.012
are all for nothing if your
users can't access them.

00:00:26.012 --> 00:00:28.470
But it's not enough to just
declare that your services must

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:29.820
be more reliable.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.520
Reliability must be a shared
goal for many different parts

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:33.900
of your organization.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.550
Today, I'm going to
start showing you

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:38.040
how you can consistently
define reliability targets

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.350
for your services using SLOs.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:43.200
Welcome to Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.110
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:48.490
The first thing
we need to define

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:50.650
is what we mean when
we say "service."

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:53.890
For our purposes, we'll mostly
refer to one of these three.

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:56.770
The first kind of service may
be an application with a user

00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:59.080
interface that's
accessed by actual humans

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:00.850
to perform various tasks.

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:03.130
We refer to those
humans as "users,"

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:05.440
and we very much care
about keeping them happy,

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:07.847
so that they continue
to use our service.

00:01:07.847 --> 00:01:09.430
We'll measure their
happiness in terms

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:13.215
of things like availability
and performance.

00:01:13.215 --> 00:01:14.590
The second kind
of service may be

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:17.350
an API, accessed by
other systems or services

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:19.540
to send or retrieve information.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:22.960
We may refer to the systems that
use our service as "consumers."

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.640
Their happiness
is also important,

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:26.260
since this interaction
may involve

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:28.060
exchanging data
that will ultimately

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:29.920
be consumed by human users.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:31.660
Availability and
performance will, again,

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:34.670
be our key measures
of reliability.

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:38.120
The final kind of service may
be a data processing pipeline.

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:40.280
Here, we'll care about
things like the throughput

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:43.370
of the system, its ability
to process data correctly,

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:45.860
and the time that it takes
data elements to traverse

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:49.480
the entire pipeline as
measures of reliability.

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:51.160
These three types of
services can end up

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:52.618
having pretty
complex architectures

00:01:52.618 --> 00:01:53.920
and infrastructures.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:57.700
But they all share the same
key feature, reliability.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.190
If that's true, then we
should treat reliability

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:01.930
like other features
of our service.

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:04.660
We'll need to plan and design
for it, architect for it,

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:05.890
and measure it.

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:09.440
But how do we measure the
reliability of our service?

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.330
One way to measure
reliability would

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:13.670
be to use uptime, or
the percentage of time

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:15.330
when our service is up.

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:17.570
However, "up" is hard
to define and measure,

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:20.120
and for complex distributed
systems with many failure

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:22.340
modes, this is not very useful.

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:26.240
Instead, we can use an event
or interaction-based model.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:28.460
We can count the number
of times our users attempt

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:31.340
to interact with our service
over a given time window

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:33.560
and divide the number
of successful attempts

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:36.790
by that total to
get a percentage.

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:39.340
But how do we define what
counts as an interaction,

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:42.795
and what makes for a good
or successful interaction?

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:44.670
That's going to depend
on the kind of service

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:46.110
we're running and managing.

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:48.150
For example, a
website or mobile app

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:50.850
can use HTTP requests,
separated by status code,

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:52.207
as an interaction.

00:02:52.207 --> 00:02:54.290
The thing we'll actually
be counting and measuring

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:57.960
is called the Service
Level Indicator, or SLI.

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:01.710
Next, we need a definition
for "good" or "successful."

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:04.530
That will depend on what our
users expect from our service.

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:07.830
In this example, we're defining
success for the profile page

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:12.500
as receiving an HTTP
200 response code.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:14.690
Now that we've defined
what success looks like,

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:16.250
we need to define
what we'll include

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:18.860
in the total or valid
set of requests.

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:22.130
For example, we may want to
exclude 400s from our count,

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:24.680
because those are likely caused
by misconfigured clients,

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:26.480
and we don't want our
reliability measure

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:30.170
to be penalized as a result. And
now, our availability, as slide

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:32.690
definition, is complete.

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:34.730
Here are other
potential SLI types

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:36.870
you can consider using
for your service.

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:39.980
The SLI menu helps you choose
an SLI for your service

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:41.750
based on who your
users are, what

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:45.060
they expect from your
service, and how they use it.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.670
OK, we know what we're
going to use to measure

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:49.170
the reliability of our service.

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:51.570
Next, we're going to
set a reliability target

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:53.400
for our service.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:56.580
The target we set is called
the SLO, or Service Level

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:57.750
Objective.

00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:01.770
It's a combination of our SLI, a
threshold, and a window of time

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:03.390
over which it's evaluated.

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:06.150
The key thing we're after
is a target or threshold

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:10.050
above which users are happy
and below which they're not.

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:12.930
It might be tempting to
set our target at 100%,

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:15.352
but that would be impossible
to meet, and result

00:04:15.352 --> 00:04:17.519
in not being able to make
any changes to our service

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:19.029
and production.

00:04:19.029 --> 00:04:20.829
We have a simple rule
of thumb for where

00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:22.350
SLO targets should be.

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:24.240
A typical user of
the service should

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:26.220
be just about happy
with the service

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:28.590
when it operates
at those targets.

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:31.340
If the service was
any less reliable,

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:33.560
you'd no longer be meeting
their expectations,

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:35.360
and they would start
to become unhappy.

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:37.257
We call this the happiness test.

00:04:37.257 --> 00:04:38.840
When we talk about
the happiness test,

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:40.910
we say "barely
met," because there

00:04:40.910 --> 00:04:44.210
are downsides to exceeding
your reliability target, too.

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:46.130
Once your users are happy
with the reliability

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:49.735
of your service, additional
reliability has little value.

00:04:49.735 --> 00:04:51.110
Maybe your users
would be happier

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:53.510
if you built new features or
made other service improvements

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:55.927
with the resources that you're
dedicating to far exceeding

00:04:55.927 --> 00:04:58.210
your reliability targets.

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:00.730
We've defined reliability as
a key feature of our service

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:04.510
and agreed to use SLIs to
measure reliability and SLOs

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:06.430
to set reliability targets.

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:09.047
But how can we actually
do those things?

00:05:09.047 --> 00:05:11.380
Let's have a look at how to
use SLO monitoring and Cloud

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:14.620
Operations to do just that.

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:17.380
Start from the project
dashboard in the Cloud Console.

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:21.320
Go to the Monitoring
menu and select Services.

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:24.040
This will bring you to the list
of services in your Workspace.

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:25.707
As you can see, in
this example, there's

00:05:25.707 --> 00:05:26.840
a long list of services.

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:28.610
Services may be
created automatically

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:32.120
from App Engine services, GA
entities, the Istio service

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:34.130
mesh, or Anthos services.

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:36.348
You can also define your
own custom services.

00:05:36.348 --> 00:05:38.390
Let's start by figuring
out which of our services

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:39.740
are having issues right now.

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:43.610
To do that, sort the list by the
SLOs with firing alert column.

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:48.540
You can see that the default
service here is at the top.

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:50.187
Let's have a closer look at it.

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:55.630
We've defined three
SLOs for this service.

00:05:55.630 --> 00:05:57.580
There's a calendar
month response latency

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:00.460
SLO, which is currently healthy,
with plenty of error budget

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:01.630
still remaining.

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:03.340
There's a rolling
window availability

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:05.410
SLO, which is also healthy.

00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:08.230
Finally, we have a
calendar week latency SLO.

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:09.770
There's plenty of
error budget left,

00:06:09.770 --> 00:06:11.770
but it's currently
showing as unhealthy.

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:13.320
Let's dig in to see
what's going on.

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:19.840
First, we can see how our SLI
is behaving against our target.

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:22.120
In this example, we're
seeing regular periods

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:26.090
where we're having trouble
meeting that target.

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:28.370
The Error budget tab
shows us our error budget

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:30.940
burn over the selected interval.

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:32.770
This rapid rise
is a great example

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:35.560
of why using calendar-based
rather than rolling SLOs

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:36.640
carries risk.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:38.530
We recover all of
our error budget

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:40.810
at once when the calendar
period rolls over,

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:44.322
rather than by actually
increasing reliability.

00:06:44.322 --> 00:06:45.780
To further investigate
our service,

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:48.120
we give you a variety of
performance and health metrics

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:50.280
that are automatically
gathered for it.

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:54.000
Because this service was defined
based on a GKE namespace,

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:56.010
we have access to
Container, Pod, and Node

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:58.050
metrics for that namespace.

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:00.630
As an example, we can switch
to the Container Restarts

00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:04.150
and Uptime view to see
how stable our service is.

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:05.900
We can see that a
number of our containers

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:08.450
restarted right
around the same time.

00:07:08.450 --> 00:07:10.160
Further down, we
can see logs being

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:12.710
emitted by our service and
the Kubernetes entity details

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:14.270
below that.

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:16.910
This is how SLO monitoring
in Cloud Operations

00:07:16.910 --> 00:07:18.470
brings together
all the information

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:20.930
you need to measure the
reliability of your services,

00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:24.560
using SLOs and the diagnostic
and troubleshooting information

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:29.833
that you might need to identify,
triage, and mitigate issues.

00:07:29.833 --> 00:07:31.000
Thanks for joining me today.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:33.340
In this first episode, we
covered some common types

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:36.130
of services, the importance
of treating their reliability

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:39.340
as a key feature, and how to
use an interaction-based model

00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:41.090
to measure reliability.

00:07:41.090 --> 00:07:43.750
Next, we learned about using
service-level objectives

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:45.500
to set reliability targets.

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:48.400
Finally, we looked at how to
use SLO monitoring in the Cloud

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:51.490
Operations Suite to measure the
reliability of your services

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:52.780
using SLOs.

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:54.213
Thanks so much for watching.

00:07:54.213 --> 00:07:56.380
Don't forget to like and
subscribe to never miss out

00:07:56.380 --> 00:07:59.410
on more Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:00.310
See you soon.

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:03.660
[MUSIC PLAYING]

